1
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:04,365
This video will continue our
discussion about device drivers and

2
00:00:04,365 --> 00:00:07,157
we'll do a general overview
of device driver design.

3
00:00:07,157 --> 00:00:12,953
So we'll talk about common Linux device
classes that device driver fits into.

4
00:00:12,953 --> 00:00:16,247
We'll talk about security concerns
when you're writing a driver, and

5
00:00:16,247 --> 00:00:18,797
also about how kernel versioning and
licensing works.

6
00:00:18,797 --> 00:00:25,853
So devices that you'd write drivers for
belong to one of a set of classes.

7
00:00:25,853 --> 00:00:29,021
And then those classes can share
common access code and methods.

8
00:00:29,021 --> 00:00:33,097
So that applications that use those
particular classes can easily

9
00:00:33,097 --> 00:00:37,693
switch between different devices that
provide that type of class access.

10
00:00:37,693 --> 00:00:41,745
So the three common types of
classes are character device,

11
00:00:41,745 --> 00:00:45,402
character class, block class,
and network class.

12
00:00:45,402 --> 00:00:51,233
So the character device class is the one
that will focus on for our implementations

13
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:55,983
for this class, it's one of
the simpler types of device classes.

14
00:00:55,983 --> 00:00:59,131
In a character device class, everything's
accessed as a stream of bytes.

15
00:00:59,131 --> 00:01:03,429
So it looks exactly like a file
to the user space applications,

16
00:01:03,429 --> 00:01:07,984
you can open, close, read, and
write like you could from a file.

17
00:01:07,984 --> 00:01:13,178
So some examples of this would be
dev/console, which is the console that

18
00:01:13,178 --> 00:01:18,228
you'd interact with, that you'd
see in your terminal for instance.

19
00:01:18,228 --> 00:01:23,037
Or dev/ttyS0 would be interaction with
the serial port that would also be

20
00:01:23,037 --> 00:01:27,250
essentially just a stream of bytes
sent out over the serial port.

21
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:29,482
It may or may not be possible to seek or

22
00:01:29,482 --> 00:01:32,077
map memory like you can
do with real files.

23
00:01:32,077 --> 00:01:35,757
So you might not be able to access
them exactly like real files.

24
00:01:35,757 --> 00:01:37,404
But the basics of read and

25
00:01:37,404 --> 00:01:42,126
write is going to work very similar to
what you would do with a normal file.

26
00:01:42,126 --> 00:01:44,855
Second class ii block device class.

27
00:01:44,855 --> 00:01:50,301
And this is really for
disk devices that can host a file system.

28
00:01:50,301 --> 00:01:53,344
And rather than being on byte boundaries,

29
00:01:53,344 --> 00:01:58,257
the transfers that happen in
the kernel are all on block boundaries.

30
00:01:58,257 --> 00:02:02,075
And usually those blocks are 512 bytes or
larger.

31
00:02:02,075 --> 00:02:05,922
And you actually can,
through the device drivers,

32
00:02:05,922 --> 00:02:11,965
still access at less than one block sizes
and the kernel will split blocks for you.

33
00:02:11,965 --> 00:02:17,599
So the interaction from user space again
looks very similar to a normal file.

34
00:02:17,599 --> 00:02:20,582
But when you're writing the device driver,

35
00:02:20,582 --> 00:02:24,042
you would need to be accessing
the device in blocks.

36
00:02:24,042 --> 00:02:28,844
So a typical block device
would be /dev/sdc something,

37
00:02:28,844 --> 00:02:33,058
for instance,
this would be your scuzzy device and

38
00:02:33,058 --> 00:02:38,350
the a1 references a specific device and
a specific partition.

39
00:02:38,350 --> 00:02:44,235
So sda1 is often your file system for
your Linux system.

40
00:02:47,353 --> 00:02:48,648
The third class is
network interface class.

41
00:02:48,648 --> 00:02:53,450
This maybe a hardware device or it could
just be something like a loop back

42
00:02:53,450 --> 00:02:57,324
interface, a software network
interface essentially.

43
00:02:57,324 --> 00:03:02,284
And the network interface class is
slightly different because instead of

44
00:03:02,284 --> 00:03:06,290
byte or block streams,
it's going to be working on packets.

45
00:03:06,290 --> 00:03:11,303
And, instead of accessing through
/dev/ some name of a device,

46
00:03:11,303 --> 00:03:14,806
we'll just use a name
like eth0 to access it.

47
00:03:14,806 --> 00:03:19,220
So why does it not have
an entry in the file system?

48
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:23,462
It's kind of breaking our rule about
everything is a file in Linux.

49
00:03:23,462 --> 00:03:26,227
It turns out the use
case with networks and

50
00:03:26,227 --> 00:03:30,574
the way packet IO works with
verification and retransmissions.

51
00:03:30,574 --> 00:03:34,751
It doesn't really work, doesn't really map
as well to the read and write paradigm.

52
00:03:34,751 --> 00:03:37,464
So for that reason network interfaces and

53
00:03:37,464 --> 00:03:41,817
network class kind of breaks that
rule about everything is a file.

54
00:03:43,399 --> 00:03:47,430
So also included as a part of
the kernel our kernel file systems.

55
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:50,314
And those are going to sit
on top of block devices,

56
00:03:50,314 --> 00:03:53,345
map those low level block
on disc to directories or

57
00:03:53,345 --> 00:03:57,405
files that we interact with through
user space in the file system.

58
00:03:57,405 --> 00:04:02,623
And the file system is independent
of the transfer mechanism,

59
00:04:02,623 --> 00:04:05,970
it's just going to work on a block device,

60
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:10,410
it doesn't care how those
blocks are stored on disk.

61
00:04:10,410 --> 00:04:15,480
So SATA, USB or SD card,
all that is abstracted away below

62
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:21,486
in the block layer and the kernel
file system is independent of that.

63
00:04:21,486 --> 00:04:25,866
The device and the device drivers
going to actually perform the transfer to

64
00:04:25,866 --> 00:04:30,908
correspond to whatever hardware you're
ultimately storing your file system on.

65
00:04:32,981 --> 00:04:36,732
So when we're talking about kernel code,
it's important to talk about security.

66
00:04:36,732 --> 00:04:41,443
The kernel can be, because it's
running at a higher privilege level,

67
00:04:41,443 --> 00:04:43,809
it's a critical point of exploit.

68
00:04:43,809 --> 00:04:48,428
If you mess up in your kernel code,
you have the potential for essentially

69
00:04:48,428 --> 00:04:53,440
giving it hacker complete access,
unrestricted access to the entire system.

70
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:58,471
So it's important that only
an authorized user can load modules.

71
00:04:58,471 --> 00:05:02,356
And then it's important that the driver
author, as a device driver author,

72
00:05:02,356 --> 00:05:06,422
you take extra special care when you're
writing your code to ensure that you're

73
00:05:06,422 --> 00:05:09,503
not going to introduce some
security problem into the system.

74
00:05:09,503 --> 00:05:13,916
So usually the drivers don't
encode security policy.

75
00:05:13,916 --> 00:05:18,674
In other words,
they don't require authentication or

76
00:05:18,674 --> 00:05:22,227
some special permission to do some role.

77
00:05:22,227 --> 00:05:26,048
Usually the device endpoint is
going to have specific read, write,

78
00:05:26,048 --> 00:05:27,695
execute permissions on it.

79
00:05:27,695 --> 00:05:31,320
And that's the way the security
policy would be handled and

80
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:32,998
set up by the system admin.

81
00:05:32,998 --> 00:05:36,629
There are some exceptions to this,
the book talks about like for

82
00:05:36,629 --> 00:05:40,194
instance, interaction with interrupts or
firmware update,

83
00:05:40,194 --> 00:05:42,969
something that could
affect the entire system.

84
00:05:42,969 --> 00:05:46,572
There are ways that device driver can
check to make sure that the user is

85
00:05:46,572 --> 00:05:48,112
the right user for instance.

86
00:05:48,112 --> 00:05:53,016
But that's typically not where that is
enforced from device driver perspective.

87
00:05:53,016 --> 00:05:57,369
So several of the security best
practices that you need to keep in mind

88
00:05:57,369 --> 00:05:59,848
when you're writing kernel drivers,

89
00:05:59,848 --> 00:06:04,672
you don't want to trust anything that
user data sends without verifying it.

90
00:06:04,672 --> 00:06:10,190
So you especially want to watch out for
cases where the user could be sending

91
00:06:10,190 --> 00:06:15,984
content that would write areas of memory
that should not be allowed to write.

92
00:06:15,984 --> 00:06:20,798
So one way that can happen is
with a buffer that's too long,

93
00:06:20,798 --> 00:06:27,461
that you copy based on a null terminator
and you would implement a buffer overrun.

94
00:06:27,461 --> 00:06:31,820
You should also be cognizant of zeroing
the memory that's obtained in the kernel

95
00:06:31,820 --> 00:06:33,369
before you pass it outside.

96
00:06:33,369 --> 00:06:38,471
When you just ask the kernel for a block
of memory and you don't zero before that

97
00:06:38,471 --> 00:06:43,594
block might have been used by some other
process to store something like a key.

98
00:06:43,594 --> 00:06:48,295
And if you just blindly return it to
the user, their security exploits where

99
00:06:48,295 --> 00:06:52,409
unprivileged application can just
keep asking for memory over and

100
00:06:52,409 --> 00:06:56,383
over again until it finally get
something that looks like a key.

101
00:06:56,383 --> 00:07:01,791
And this could be a away that a hacker
could get access into your system.

102
00:07:02,883 --> 00:07:08,067
So when we talk about buffer
overruns what you really mean

103
00:07:08,067 --> 00:07:14,336
is a way that we can send executable
code as a part of a user operation.

104
00:07:14,336 --> 00:07:21,332
And overwrite the content that would
ultimately be used as a return address,

105
00:07:21,332 --> 00:07:26,077
2.2 instead this user code
that can be executed.

106
00:07:26,077 --> 00:07:30,494
So there's an example here in user
space about how this can happen.

107
00:07:30,494 --> 00:07:35,004
So you can see in this dangerous function,
the main function here

108
00:07:35,004 --> 00:07:39,603
takes an argument and just passes
it into the dangerous function.

109
00:07:39,603 --> 00:07:44,315
And you can see the dangerous function
here creates a array that's called

110
00:07:44,315 --> 00:07:48,276
too small and then just string
copies in whatever was passed.

111
00:07:48,276 --> 00:07:53,257
So if you pass in a string to
this function that's longer than

112
00:07:53,257 --> 00:07:58,056
100 bytes you'll overwrite
that too small variable.

113
00:07:58,056 --> 00:08:02,597
And based on the way that
stack allocates that variable,

114
00:08:02,597 --> 00:08:05,729
you may overwrite the return address.

115
00:08:05,729 --> 00:08:11,288
So what a carefully crafted
string in this case,

116
00:08:11,288 --> 00:08:17,255
could actually contain
a chunk of malicious code and

117
00:08:17,255 --> 00:08:21,609
could overwrite the return address.

118
00:08:21,609 --> 00:08:26,461
Such that instead of returning from
the dangerous function when what should

119
00:08:26,461 --> 00:08:29,871
have been the return addresses
pulled off the stack,

120
00:08:29,871 --> 00:08:34,829
will instead pull off a return address
that points us back inside the string.

121
00:08:34,829 --> 00:08:40,064
It has some type of often a NOP sled
in other words, a series of no ops just

122
00:08:40,064 --> 00:08:45,237
to help make sure that you're going to
be able to hit the malicious code.

123
00:08:45,237 --> 00:08:50,232
And a section of malicious code
that's crafted to do something that

124
00:08:50,232 --> 00:08:52,169
it's not supposed to do.

125
00:08:52,169 --> 00:08:56,094
So this is just an example of what
you need to be careful of and

126
00:08:56,094 --> 00:09:01,041
the reason you need to care about watching
things like links of content that

127
00:09:01,041 --> 00:09:05,223
you get provided from the user
before you use it in your driver.

128
00:09:05,223 --> 00:09:07,572
[MUSIC]

129
00:09:07,572 --> 00:09:12,361
So the book has a whole section on
kernel versioning and where there wasn't

130
00:09:12,361 --> 00:09:16,705
even odd numbering scheme for
the kernel that was used previously.

131
00:09:16,705 --> 00:09:21,837
And that is no longer in use and
now uses a simpler major.minor number and

132
00:09:21,837 --> 00:09:24,290
it's not feature based anymore.

133
00:09:24,290 --> 00:09:27,672
All the releases that are done on
the Linux kernel are based on time.

134
00:09:27,672 --> 00:09:31,979
And basically simpler scheme
that's discussed in the kernel and

135
00:09:31,979 --> 00:09:34,806
if you look at this
mailing list post here.

136
00:09:34,806 --> 00:09:38,218
Linus Torvalds said that the reason he
did it was because he was running out of

137
00:09:38,218 --> 00:09:39,085
fingers and toes.

138
00:09:39,085 --> 00:09:44,265
So this is just a difference between
what's written in the book and

139
00:09:44,265 --> 00:09:48,213
how Linux kernel versioning
actually works today.

140
00:09:48,213 --> 00:09:52,846
We've also talked about licensing
as it applies to Linux kernel.

141
00:09:52,846 --> 00:09:58,302
So the Linux kernel in general is
licensed with new general public license,

142
00:09:58,302 --> 00:09:59,590
GPL version two.

143
00:09:59,590 --> 00:10:03,215
So that means that anyone can
redistribute and you can sell and

144
00:10:03,215 --> 00:10:06,572
make money off a product that
includes the Linux kernel.

145
00:10:06,572 --> 00:10:10,758
As long as the recipient of your
product has access to the source and

146
00:10:10,758 --> 00:10:13,616
has the ability to
exercise the same rights.

147
00:10:13,616 --> 00:10:18,188
So in other words,
they're able to take the source code

148
00:10:18,188 --> 00:10:22,177
that you use to build your kernel and
modify it and

149
00:10:22,177 --> 00:10:28,032
you make their own changes to it and
build their own product based on it.

150
00:10:28,032 --> 00:10:32,516
So, any software products derived
from a product covered by the GPL,

151
00:10:32,516 --> 00:10:36,316
if it's redistributed,
must also be released under GPL.

152
00:10:36,316 --> 00:10:41,480
And this is what we talked about before
with the copy left type of license

153
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:46,058
where it's going to apply not
only to you as you're developing.

154
00:10:46,058 --> 00:10:50,781
But it has implications on
the additional code that you're

155
00:10:50,781 --> 00:10:55,324
adding also needs to be licensed
in this particular way.

156
00:10:57,143 --> 00:10:59,997
So the book goes over the reason for
this and

157
00:10:59,997 --> 00:11:02,864
the idea was to allow growth of knowledge.

158
00:11:02,864 --> 00:11:08,603
Basically enforce the requirement
that this code is shared and

159
00:11:08,603 --> 00:11:13,914
that it would be essentially
given back to the community.

160
00:11:13,914 --> 00:11:16,198
So that others can see the changes and

161
00:11:16,198 --> 00:11:20,028
that there would be some level
of collaboration happening and

162
00:11:20,028 --> 00:11:24,830
growing this common code base, but
also still allowing for commercial use.

163
00:11:24,830 --> 00:11:29,416
So, the book also talks about
whether a kernel module can be

164
00:11:29,416 --> 00:11:31,626
shared only in binary form.

165
00:11:31,626 --> 00:11:36,729
And this is actually something that's
been left deliberately ambiguous.

166
00:11:36,729 --> 00:11:41,362
But what the book also mentions
is that there are a lot of

167
00:11:41,362 --> 00:11:45,413
ways that binary kernel
modules can be broken.

168
00:11:45,413 --> 00:11:50,276
And kernel developers have
no real motivation to try

169
00:11:50,276 --> 00:11:54,375
to keep binary drivers working correctly.

170
00:11:54,375 --> 00:11:58,952
And so you should kind of expect
an uphill battle if you wanted to release

171
00:11:58,952 --> 00:12:00,720
the driver in binary form.

172
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:04,106
And beyond that, you need to really
understand the legal ramifications.

173
00:12:04,106 --> 00:12:07,542
And so the other suggestion is if you,
for some reason,

174
00:12:07,542 --> 00:12:12,298
did want to release a kernel module in
binary form, you should definitely seek

175
00:12:12,298 --> 00:12:16,566
legal advice to confirm that you can
meet the obligations of the GPL.